Conditional **κ = 0.001676896669021299** on PR1319's unchanged strict T74 chain, **+4.144e-8** over PR1319 (0.001676855229071321).

Stacked with PR1549's 200-digit refinement of the same assembly, the value is **κ = 0.0016768966690212993077507109972679626716114900163391596106…**. That run uses PR1549's assembler, vendored unchanged, with this construction's own 1e-200 certified endpoints. It sits 4.144e-8 above PR1549 and above the later digit refinements (#1574, #1578, #1582, #1601), all of which keep PR1319's construction unchanged.

**Revision 2 (2026-10-14)** answers the batch review's continuation point
([comment](https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/pull/1625#issuecomment-6172497784)):
- the restoration argument over the whole audited program, written up in `RESTORATION.md` and checked exactly by
  `kernel_check.py` in the registered hook;
- PR1319's own admission of the unchanged ancestor, with regenerated containers bound by payload (PR1319's recorded
  blocker is gzip bytes), plus container and payload controls;
- a fresh side-by-side replay of the ancestor and the kernel, natives and whole-call T74 chain included, compared by
  `interfaces.py`.

Details are in the README section *Revision 2*.

**What changes.** Only the two local words change: PR1297's gauge44 and extra words, as vendored in PR1319. The edit is a seven-insert helper-donor kernel for cold pivot q = 566:
- delete the early read B755 (`494 -= q`);
- open a helper window `98 -= q` before B520 and restore it with `98 += q` at early-cleanup position 86, right after the gate that restores q;
- add donor windows on 590 (−q / +q, before B0 and B47) and on 672 (−2q / +2q, before B0 and B224);
- add direct reads `150 += q` and `209 -= q`.

Every new read sees q's initial value, so only q's column can change, and the nine terms cancel exactly (RESTORATION.md). The derived compensation and the whole program map are unchanged, and the interfaces are byte-identical. With optimal frames for the toolkit's integer weights, each call changes by H1 +1, H2 −2, H3 +1 on both words; that is rank-mass neutral and saves 1,855 cost units. PR1319's owners, dispatch (249/417), parent, construction recipe and idle remainder are reused byte-for-byte.

**Verification** (local, macOS arm64):
- `python3.12 scripts/research_package.py --package research/t74-pivot566-kernel` gives `PASS_REGISTERED_RESEARCH_PACKAGE research/t74-pivot566-kernel outcome=completed`, also from a clean checkout of the head. Its bounded `verify.py` takes about 5 s and checks:
  - all input pins, including all 149 PR1319 files against PR1319's MANIFEST;
  - an exact rebuild of both shipped words from PR1297's pinned words;
  - the restoration argument (`kernel_check.py`: lemma, nine-term ledger, compensation, frame recount, four mutated kernels rejected), which must reproduce `evidence/KERNEL-CHECK.json`;
  - an unchanged `audit_local.py` pass, with recounted profiles equal to the PLAN;
  - κ recomputed from the profile with the retained moment and outer engines;
  - binding of the recorded receipts, including the fresh side-by-side replay;
  - five failure controls (flipped coefficient, dropped restore, read in the busy window, corrupted byte, mass-neutral forged profile), all rejected.
- `replay_full.py --refined` runs the complete chain fresh, with no native reuse, in 792 s of stages (`evidence/FULL-REPLAY.json`):
  - PR1297's own admission, with fresh natives of its original words;
  - PR1319's own admission of the unchanged ancestor: reconstruct and lift with regenerated containers bound by payload, container and payload controls, then its strict T74 replay (`PASS_STRICT_MIXED_T74_EXACT_FILL 0.001676855229071321`) and 6 geometry controls;
  - fresh lineage natives of both kernel words, and a rebind that reproduces `data/PLAN.json`;
  - PR1319's strict replay on the kernel, `PASS_STRICT_MIXED_T74_EXACT_FILL 0.001676896669021299`, with 114 admission, 12 bit, 8 owner, 6 geometry and 4 outer controls rejected, 47 outer margins positive, 319,680 roles, prefix 1,441 of 2,048 charged bits and row coefficient 19,545 < 20,161;
  - `kernel_check.py` and `interfaces.py`. Call interfaces are identical, and every hosting quantity of the two strict replays is equal (`evidence/INTERFACES.json`);
  - PR1549's fine-engine oracle and the refined strict replay, which passes.
- The repository's research tests (assignment and onboarding) pass.

**Independent check.** philippeVV reviewed revision 2 ([review](https://github.com/CrocSwap/integer-mult-bounds/pull/1625#issuecomment-6179118662)) and found no defect in the finite kernel. They re-ran `replay_full.py --refined` from scratch with no native reuse (all 15 stages pass, 1,431 s) and the registered hook. Their own simulator, which uses none of this PR's code, finds the whole-program map equal to the ancestor's and to the call map on both words (0 differing rows). They also confirm that the edit is exactly the seven B inserts, the deleted read and the one cleanup gate, and that `audit_local.py` passes. The inherited T74 supplier, all-size and outer obligations remain open, as stated under Scope.

**Stacking and overlap.** The branch is stacked on PR1319's head `603defe5`, so it includes PR1319's two commits until that merges. No open PR or discussion mentions a pivot-566 kernel or any other kernel after PR1297. Every κ claim built on PR1319 refines digits only. Their owners, plan, template, dispatch, profile and words are byte-identical, so this kernel stacks on all of them. The PR1337 and PR1338 censuses searched only the restricted two-shock family; this kernel lies outside it.

**Scope.** This is a conditional finite construction. The inherited assumptions remain: all-size compiler, common-label/mutable-source realization, whole-call hosting, precision/tape, exact recovery, analytic transfer, PR559 recurrence correction and PR589 bit H1–H5. There is no Lean proof and no formal verification of the multiplication theorem. The frames come from the PR1186 solver; `audit_local.py` and the native chain validate them. Hosted CI is pending.

**Open next questions** (README, TASK.json):
- remaining written cold pivots with the same kernel shape;
- two-column (port re-timing) edits for the never-written copy-window pivots;
- independent re-derivation of the frames.

